ook at is  the Russian variation and it's probably  the most aggressive from white so if  you're playing white and you see you do  see the green fell defense and you still  want to keep that aggressive nature in  you then I suggest the Russian variation  because it is very aggressive from white  it gets the Queen involved early and  therefore you're going to be putting a  lot of pressure on black and it  continues with the moves Knight to f3  instead of capturing at the beginning or  trying to gain center control with your  Bishop you're instead going to bring  your knight to f3 if he brings his  Bishop into the act your that's not  going to be pinned down so more times  than not you're not going to see the  bishop come out right away instead more  times than that you'll see the bishop  being ketta to g7 and from here the  Queen can now come to b3 and this is the  aggressive move that we're talking about  here we're pretty much forcing black to  capture on c4 and after he captures and  we recapture with our queen on c4  you can already tell that we don't quite  have the same presence in the middle  with our pawns as we did in the exchange  variation but at the same time we do  still have a very good center control we  have good center control with our  Knights we have a good pawn placed on d4  and our Queens very active now a lot of  times black will try to chase our coin  around but this is why this is the more  aggressive variation so for those white  players that are aggressive keep in mind  this is an option for if you want to  avoid the exchange variation or bring  your Bishop to f4 which is very popular  and I like so this is the groom fel  defense take a look at it play around  with it let me know what you think  everyone thanks for watching  if you haven't already please subscribe  if you haven't checked out the website  please do WWH s website com let me know  what you think and I'll see you guys  next video thanks for watching everybody
 

